## 13. Phase Eleven — Inventory and Multi-Vendor Allocation

Preserve the existing multi-vendor allocation rules and repair any problems found during the audit.

Requirements:

- Maintain stock per vendor and product variant.
- Calculate eligible combined stock accurately.
- Consider vendor service area and customer locality.
- Use configured pricing, delivery charges, route and pickup costs, and applicable business rules.
- Prefer one vendor when it can fulfil the order optimally.
- Split orders when the defined allocation rules justify doing so.
- Do not hardcode vendor stock, prices, delivery rates, or allocation results.
- Revalidate stock and prices at checkout.
- Prevent overselling under concurrent requests.
- Record parent orders and vendor suborders consistently.

Use PostgreSQL transactions and appropriate row locking or conditional updates for inventory operations.

For example, when reducing inventory, verify that sufficient stock remains and apply the decrement within the same transaction. If any part of checkout fails, roll back the relevant changes.

Record reservations, releases, deductions, and reversals in an auditable stock movement ledger.

Do not deduct stock twice. Do not release a reservation twice. Do not treat an expired reservation as available if the corresponding order has been confirmed.

Use idempotency keys or equivalent safeguards for order creation and payment-related retries.

If a stock update succeeds but a later order operation fails, use transaction rollback rather than leaving inconsistent data.

